+
+## Validation Notes
+
+**Iteration 1 (2026-11-16)** — initial draft reviewed; two issues found and fixed, one
+documented deviation retained.
+
+1. *Implementation detail in the body* — the first draft named Python, `http.server`, and the
+   `src/pastebin` layout inside the functional requirements. Fixed: all platform- and
+   layout-imposed facts were moved into a separate **Constraints** section, clearly labelled as
+   operator-imposed rather than derived from user need. User Scenarios, Requirements, Success
+   Criteria, and Key Entities are now free of language and framework references.
+
+2. *Missing security section* — the project constitution (Principle V and the Security Baseline)
+   requires every specification to state its security requirements alongside its functional ones,
+   and a spec without that section must be returned. Fixed: added **Security Requirements**
+   (SR-001 … SR-012), each mapped to an OWASP Top 10 / ASVS control and each written to be
+   testable with negative tests.
+
+**Documented deviation — HTTP status codes in the Requirements section.** FR-010, FR-019,
+FR-020, FR-021, and FR-026 … FR-028 name specific response codes (`201`, `204`, `400`, `403`,
+`404`, `413`). Strictly these are protocol details. They are retained deliberately because:
+
+- They were specified exactly by the requester and form the externally observable contract this
+  feature is judged against — in particular the rule that not-found takes precedence over
+  forbidden, which is only expressible in those terms.
+- Paraphrasing them away ("report a permission failure") would make the requirements
+  untestable against the agreed contract, failing constitution Principle III.
+
+The **Success Criteria** section is kept free of them, so the measurable outcomes remain
+technology-agnostic and verifiable from the user's point of view. The user stories and
+acceptance scenarios likewise describe outcomes in plain language.
+
+## Coverage Check
+
+- **Clarification markers**: 0. Every gap in the input was resolved with a documented default in
+  the Assumptions section rather than deferred — notably the handling of unsupported actions on
+  known addresses, timestamp precision, expired-record cleanup, and the out-of-scope boundary
+  (no listing, no editing, no web interface).
+- **Constitution alignment**: Principle I is recorded under Constraints; Principle II (fail
+  loudly) is covered by FR-004 and FR-025 … FR-028; Principle III by SC-008, which requires every
+  FR and SR identifier to map to a named automated test; Principle IV by the explicit
+  out-of-scope assumptions; Principle V by SR-001 … SR-012 and SC-008 … SC-010.
+- **Security Baseline coverage**: untrusted input (SR-004), no string-built sinks (SR-003,
+  SR-012), path containment (SR-003), credential storage — not applicable, no passwords are
+  stored; revocation secrets are random capabilities protected by SR-008 and SR-009 —
+  randomness and comparison (SR-001, SR-002), resource limits (SR-005, FR-029), access control
+  (SR-006, SR-007), file permissions (SR-008), output redaction (SR-009).
+
+## Notes
